# Refresh the login-banner dongle-MAC line when a USB-Ethernet dongle is
# plugged or unplugged, so the banner tracks hotplug, not just boot-time state.
#
# ⚠ Uses ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}, NOT RUN+="systemctl ...". RUN+= holds the udev
# worker until the command returns, and `systemctl` needs a D-Bus round trip to
# PID1 — so whenever PID1 is busy, a cosmetic banner refresh turns into a wedged
# udev worker. That is not hypothetical: on 34291FDHS000WV 2026-08-05, staging a
# 4 GB OTA image starved PID1, a dongle re-enumeration landed mid-transfer, and
#     Spawned '/bin/systemctl --no-block restart dongle-mac-issue.service'
#     is taking longer than 59s to complete
# extended the stall until softdog reset the device at 120s — which the
# bootloader then reported as `0xbaba - Kernel PANIC`, i.e. our own banner rule
# helped turn a healthy mid-OTA device into an apparent kernel crash.
# (`--no-block` does not save you: it only skips waiting for the JOB, the
# connect-and-enqueue to PID1 still blocks.)
#
# SYSTEMD_WANTS hands the unit to systemd as a property of the device and lets
# the worker exit immediately, so a busy PID1 can never wedge udev here. The
# trade is that it starts rather than restarts the unit, so the oneshot needs
# RemainAfterExit=no to re-run on each hotplug — see dongle-mac-issue.service.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="net", ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb", \
  ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="dongle-mac-issue.service"

# `remove` carries no SYSTEMD_WANTS semantics (the device is going away, so
# there is nothing for systemd to bind the unit to). Use systemd-run, which
# forks and returns immediately instead of waiting on a D-Bus job like systemctl.
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="net", ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --unit=dongle-mac-issue-refresh /usr/local/sbin/dongle-mac-issue"
